{"sec":"A","topic":"coding","diff":3,"q":"In a code, odd-position letters move +2 and even-position letters move −2 (positions start at 1). CRIME becomes:","opts":["EMIRC","EPKKG","ETKOG","APGKC"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: some codes apply a DIFFERENT rule depending on where a letter sits in the word — always read the position rule first (odd vs even) before touching any letter, since applying one uniform shift to the whole word is the classic trap. Step 1: number each letter's position in CRIME starting at 1, and note whether it's odd or even. Step 2: shift odd-position letters forward by 2 and even-position letters backward by 2 — never apply the same shift to all letters (that's the trap in the distractors). Step 3: letter by letter: C(pos1)→E R(pos2)→P I(pos3)→K M(pos4)→K E(pos5)→G, giving EPKKG. Exam tip: mark each letter with a small 'o' or 'e' above it for odd/even position before shifting — this prevents mixing up which direction applies where.","tags":["coding"],"id":"R377"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"arith_reasoning","diff":3,"q":"The angle between the hands of a clock at 4:30 is:","opts":["30°","75°","45°","60°"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: a clock's hour hand and minute hand each sweep the dial at their own constant speed (30° per hour, 5.5° per minute), so the angle between them at any exact time can be read straight off the formula |30×Hour − 5.5×Minute| — no need to visualise the clock face. Step 1: use the clock-angle formula: angle = |30×Hour − 5.5×Minute|. Step 2: substitute H=4, M=30: |30×4 − 5.5×30| = |120 − 165|. Step 3: 120 − 165 = -45, so the angle is 45° (taking the smaller angle if it exceeds 180°). Exam tip: if your answer comes out above 180°, subtract it from 360° — a clock always reports the SMALLER of the two possible angles between the hands.","tags":["arith_reasoning"],"id":"R378"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"operations","diff":2,"q":"If '+' means '×' and '−' means '÷', then 14 + 7 = ?","opts":["7","105","98","21"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: symbol-substitution questions swap the MEANING of operators, not the numbers — before calculating anything, rewrite the whole expression replacing every symbol with what it actually now stands for. Step 1: rewrite the expression using the REAL operation each symbol stands for — here '+' secretly means '×'. Step 2: the expression 14 + 7 becomes 14 × 7 once rewritten. Step 3: calculate: 14 × 7 = 98. The distractor 21 comes from forgetting to swap the symbol and adding normally instead. Exam tip: physically cross out each original symbol and write its replacement above it before doing any arithmetic — this stops your brain from defaulting to the familiar meaning.","tags":["operations"],"id":"R379"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"operations","diff":2,"q":"If '+' means '×' and '−' means '÷', then 13 + 7 = ?","opts":["98","91","20","6"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: symbol-substitution questions swap the MEANING of operators, not the numbers — before calculating anything, rewrite the whole expression replacing every symbol with what it actually now stands for. Step 1: rewrite the expression using the REAL operation each symbol stands for — here '+' secretly means '×'. Step 2: the expression 13 + 7 becomes 13 × 7 once rewritten. Step 3: calculate: 13 × 7 = 91. The distractor 20 comes from forgetting to swap the symbol and adding normally instead. Exam tip: physically cross out each original symbol and write its replacement above it before doing any arithmetic — this stops your brain from defaulting to the familiar meaning.","tags":["operations"],"id":"R380"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"operations","diff":3,"q":"If '−' means '+', '+' means '×2 of the second number added', solve mentally: interchange '×' and '+' in  23 × 8 = ?  (i.e., compute 23 + 8)","opts":["31","15","33","184"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: interchange-of-symbols questions ask you to literally swap the roles of two operators before solving — read the instruction twice and rewrite the expression with operators swapped BEFORE any calculation. Step 1: interchange the operations exactly as instructed — swap what '×' and '+' represent in this question. Step 2: after interchanging, the required calculation reduces to a straightforward addition: 23 + 8. Step 3: 23 + 8 = 31. The distractor 184 comes from forgetting to interchange and multiplying directly instead. Exam tip: these questions bank on your instinct to compute the expression as printed — always rewrite it symbol-swapped on paper first, don't try to do the swap purely in your head.","tags":["operations"],"id":"R381"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"series","diff":2,"q":"4, 6, 10, 16, 24, ?","opts":["33","36","32","34"],"ans":3,"exp":"Principle: when even the first-level differences change, don't give up — take the difference OF the differences (a second layer); if that second layer is constant, the series has a steadily changing step you can still predict. Step 1: the plain differences aren't constant — 2, 4, 6, 8 — so check if THOSE differences follow a pattern. Step 2: the differences themselves increase by 2 each time (2, 4, 6...). Step 3: the next difference is 10; add it to the last term: 24 + 10 = 34. Exam tip: write the difference row directly under the series on your rough sheet — seeing it as its own line makes the second-level pattern easy to spot.","tags":["series"],"id":"R382"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"series","diff":2,"q":"2, 5, 11, 20, 32, ?","opts":["47","50","44","46"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: when even the first-level differences change, don't give up — take the difference OF the differences (a second layer); if that second layer is constant, the series has a steadily changing step you can still predict. Step 1: the plain differences aren't constant — 3, 6, 9, 12 — so check if THOSE differences follow a pattern. Step 2: the differences themselves increase by 3 each time (3, 6, 9...). Step 3: the next difference is 15; add it to the last term: 32 + 15 = 47. Exam tip: write the difference row directly under the series on your rough sheet — seeing it as its own line makes the second-level pattern easy to spot.","tags":["series"],"id":"R383"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"matching","diff":2,"q":"Exactly ONE of the four options matches the given text perfectly. Find it.\nGiven: Vikram Raj Slathia, Sec 1, Channi Himmat, Jammu 180015","opts":["Vikram Raj Slathia, Sec 1, Channi Himmat, Jammu 980015","Vikram Raj Slathia, Sec 1, Channi Himmat, Jammu 180015","Vikram Raj Slathia, Sec 1, Channi Himmat, Jammu 480015","Vikram Raj Slathiv, Sec 1, Channi Himmat, Jammu 180015"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: address/name-matching questions rely on your eye skipping over a single altered character in a long string — the fix is to never scan the whole line at once, but to break it into small, independently-checkable chunks. Step 1: split the text into chunks (name, street, city, PIN code) rather than reading it as one block. Step 2: compare each chunk of every option against the given text, one chunk at a time. Step 3: the three wrong options each have exactly ONE character changed (a swapped digit or letter, often hidden in the PIN code) — the option with zero changes anywhere is the match. Exam tip: check the PIN code digit-by-digit last, since numeric swaps there are the easiest changes to miss when skimming quickly.","tags":["matching"],"id":"R384"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"analogies","diff":2,"q":"B : D :: S : ?","opts":["Q","V","T","U"],"ans":3,"exp":"Principle: letter analogies usually encode a fixed forward or backward jump along A-to-Z — treat each letter as its position number (A=1...Z=26), measure the jump once, and repeat it identically. Step 1: find how far B moved to become D in the alphabet — count forward: it's +2 positions. Step 2: verify there's no shorter/alternate explanation (e.g. reverse-order or vowel-consonant swap) — a simple shift fits perfectly. Step 3: apply the same +2 shift to S: S + 2 positions = U. The distractors use a wrong shift amount or the wrong direction (−2 instead of +2). Exam tip: watch for wraparound near Z→A — count positions on your fingers or write the alphabet strip if the shift crosses that boundary.","tags":["analogies"],"id":"R385"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"analogies","diff":2,"q":"F : H :: J : ?","opts":["M","H","L","K"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: letter analogies usually encode a fixed forward or backward jump along A-to-Z — treat each letter as its position number (A=1...Z=26), measure the jump once, and repeat it identically. Step 1: find how far F moved to become H in the alphabet — count forward: it's +2 positions. Step 2: verify there's no shorter/alternate explanation (e.g. reverse-order or vowel-consonant swap) — a simple shift fits perfectly. Step 3: apply the same +2 shift to J: J + 2 positions = L. The distractors use a wrong shift amount or the wrong direction (−2 instead of +2). Exam tip: watch for wraparound near Z→A — count positions on your fingers or write the alphabet strip if the shift crosses that boundary.","tags":["analogies"],"id":"R386"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"analogies","diff":2,"q":"7 : 49 :: 4 : ?","opts":["20","16","14","18"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: number analogies swap the 'word relationship' idea for a single arithmetic operation — find what was done to the first number to get the second, then do the exact same sum to the third number. Step 1: test the pair 7 : 49 against simple operations (add, multiply, square) until one fits — here it is 'square'. Step 2: confirm: 7 → square → 49, correct. Step 3: apply the identical operation to 4: 4 → square → 16. The wrong options come from applying a different or partial operation, not the confirmed rule. Exam tip: always test in the order add → multiply → square, and verify with the exact numbers given rather than assuming the first operation that comes to mind.","tags":["analogies"],"id":"R387"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"space","diff":2,"q":"A man faces West. He turns right, then left, then right. Which direction does he face now?","opts":["North","South","East","West"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: direction-sense turning questions become trivial once you fix the compass as a circular ring N→E→S→W→N — a right (clockwise) turn always moves one step forward around this ring, a left (anticlockwise) turn always moves one step back, regardless of how many turns are chained. Step 1: sketch the compass ring N→E→S→W→N. A right turn moves one step forward in this ring; a left turn moves one step backward. Step 2: starting at West, apply each turn in order: right turn → now facing North; left turn → now facing West; right turn → now facing North. Step 3: after all turns, he faces North. Exam tip: for multiple 90° turns, track only the CURRENT facing after each turn — never try to hold the whole sequence in your head at once, since that's where errors creep in.","tags":["space"],"id":"R388"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"coding","diff":1,"q":"If a word is written backwards, ALERT becomes:","opts":["TRELA","USFMB","ATREL","LERTA"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: 'written backwards' means exactly one thing — read the letters in reverse order — it is not a shift, a rotation, or any other coding trick, so no letter-value math is needed here. Step 1: identify the rule stated — 'written backwards' means simple letter-order reversal, not a shift or rotation. Step 2: write out ALERT's letters in reverse order one by one: T R E L A. Step 3: join them to get TRELA — the wrong options instead rotate the first letter to the end or apply an unrelated shift. Exam tip: write the original word and then physically write its letters right-to-left below it — this catches accidental rotation errors instantly.","tags":["coding"],"id":"R389"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"matching","diff":2,"q":"Exactly ONE of the four options matches the given text perfectly. Find it.\nGiven: Mohd Ashraf Bhat, H.No 42, Sec 3, Trikuta Nagar, Jammu 180012","opts":["Mohd Ashraf Bhat, H.No 42, Sec 3, Trisuta Nagar, Jammu 180012","Mohd Ashraf Bhat, H.No 42, Sec 3, Trikuta Nagar, Jammu 180012","Mohd Ashraf Bhat, H.No 42, Sec 3, Trikuta Nagar, Jammj 180012","Mohd Ashraf Bhat, H.No 42, Sec 3, Trikuta Nagar, Jamuu 180012"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: address/name-matching questions rely on your eye skipping over a single altered character in a long string — the fix is to never scan the whole line at once, but to break it into small, independently-checkable chunks. Step 1: split the text into chunks (name, street, city, PIN code) rather than reading it as one block. Step 2: compare each chunk of every option against the given text, one chunk at a time. Step 3: the three wrong options each have exactly ONE character changed (a swapped digit or letter, often hidden in the PIN code) — the option with zero changes anywhere is the match. Exam tip: check the PIN code digit-by-digit last, since numeric swaps there are the easiest changes to miss when skimming quickly.","tags":["matching"],"id":"R390"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"matching","diff":2,"q":"Exactly ONE of the four options matches the given text perfectly. Find it.\nGiven: Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Lane 7, Rajbagh, Srinagar 190008","opts":["Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Lane 7, Rajbbgh, Srinagar 190008","Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Lane 7, Rajbagh, Srinagaj 190008","Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Lane 7, Rajbagh, Srinagar 190008","Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Lane 7, Rabbagh, Srinagar 190008"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: address/name-matching questions rely on your eye skipping over a single altered character in a long string — the fix is to never scan the whole line at once, but to break it into small, independently-checkable chunks. Step 1: split the text into chunks (name, street, city, PIN code) rather than reading it as one block. Step 2: compare each chunk of every option against the given text, one chunk at a time. Step 3: the three wrong options each have exactly ONE character changed (a swapped digit or letter, often hidden in the PIN code) — the option with zero changes anywhere is the match. Exam tip: check the PIN code digit-by-digit last, since numeric swaps there are the easiest changes to miss when skimming quickly.","tags":["matching"],"id":"R391"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"arith_reasoning","diff":3,"q":"A farm has hens and cows: 7 heads and 18 legs in all. How many cows are there?","opts":["3","1","2","5"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: classic 'heads and legs' puzzles are solved by the assumption method — pretend every animal is the type with fewer legs, see how far short the leg count falls, and each 'wrong' animal you swap in makes up a fixed, known amount of the shortfall. Step 1: assume ALL 7 animals were hens (2 legs each) — that would give 14 legs total. Step 2: the actual leg count is 18, which is 4 MORE than the all-hens assumption — this excess comes from cows having 2 extra legs each. Step 3: number of cows = excess legs ÷ 2 = 4 ÷ 2 = 2. Exam tip: this 'assume all of one kind' trick works for any two-type heads-and-legs (or price-mix) problem — always assume the type with the SMALLER per-unit value first, so the excess is always positive.","tags":["arith_reasoning"],"id":"R392"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"space","diff":2,"q":"A man faces South. He turns right, then right. Which direction does he face now?","opts":["South","East","West","North"],"ans":3,"exp":"Principle: direction-sense turning questions become trivial once you fix the compass as a circular ring N→E→S→W→N — a right (clockwise) turn always moves one step forward around this ring, a left (anticlockwise) turn always moves one step back, regardless of how many turns are chained. Step 1: sketch the compass ring N→E→S→W→N. A right turn moves one step forward in this ring; a left turn moves one step backward. Step 2: starting at South, apply each turn in order: right turn → now facing West; right turn → now facing North. Step 3: after all turns, he faces North. Exam tip: for multiple 90° turns, track only the CURRENT facing after each turn — never try to hold the whole sequence in your head at once, since that's where errors creep in.","tags":["space"],"id":"R393"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"series","diff":1,"q":"8, 16, 24, 32, 40, ?","opts":["40","48","56","49"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: the first thing to try on any number series is the plain difference between consecutive terms — if that difference is constant, the series is arithmetic and the next term just continues adding it. Step 1: write the difference between each pair of consecutive terms: 8, 8, 8, 8. Step 2: since all differences equal 8, this is a simple arithmetic series. Step 3: extend the pattern by adding 8 to the last term: 40 + 8 = 48. Exam tip: don't stop after checking just one gap — confirm the SAME difference repeats across all consecutive pairs before trusting it, since a single matching gap can be a coincidence.","tags":["series"],"id":"R394"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"operations","diff":3,"q":"If '−' means '+', '+' means '×2 of the second number added', solve mentally: interchange '×' and '+' in  12 × 8 = ?  (i.e., compute 12 + 8)","opts":["22","20","4","96"],"ans":1,"exp":"Principle: interchange-of-symbols questions ask you to literally swap the roles of two operators before solving — read the instruction twice and rewrite the expression with operators swapped BEFORE any calculation. Step 1: interchange the operations exactly as instructed — swap what '×' and '+' represent in this question. Step 2: after interchanging, the required calculation reduces to a straightforward addition: 12 + 8. Step 3: 12 + 8 = 20. The distractor 96 comes from forgetting to interchange and multiplying directly instead. Exam tip: these questions bank on your instinct to compute the expression as printed — always rewrite it symbol-swapped on paper first, don't try to do the swap purely in your head.","tags":["operations"],"id":"R395"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"analogies","diff":2,"q":"D : H :: Q : ?","opts":["U","M","T","V"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: letter analogies usually encode a fixed forward or backward jump along A-to-Z — treat each letter as its position number (A=1...Z=26), measure the jump once, and repeat it identically. Step 1: find how far D moved to become H in the alphabet — count forward: it's +4 positions. Step 2: verify there's no shorter/alternate explanation (e.g. reverse-order or vowel-consonant swap) — a simple shift fits perfectly. Step 3: apply the same +4 shift to Q: Q + 4 positions = U. The distractors use a wrong shift amount or the wrong direction (−4 instead of +4). Exam tip: watch for wraparound near Z→A — count positions on your fingers or write the alphabet strip if the shift crosses that boundary.","tags":["analogies"],"id":"R396"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"coding","diff":3,"q":"In a code, odd-position letters move +2 and even-position letters move −2 (positions start at 1). CHIEF becomes:","opts":["EFKCH","FEIHC","AFGCD","EJKGH"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: some codes apply a DIFFERENT rule depending on where a letter sits in the word — always read the position rule first (odd vs even) before touching any letter, since applying one uniform shift to the whole word is the classic trap. Step 1: number each letter's position in CHIEF starting at 1, and note whether it's odd or even. Step 2: shift odd-position letters forward by 2 and even-position letters backward by 2 — never apply the same shift to all letters (that's the trap in the distractors). Step 3: letter by letter: C(pos1)→E H(pos2)→F I(pos3)→K E(pos4)→C F(pos5)→H, giving EFKCH. Exam tip: mark each letter with a small 'o' or 'e' above it for odd/even position before shifting — this prevents mixing up which direction applies where.","tags":["coding"],"id":"R397"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"series","diff":1,"q":"H, J, L, N, P, ?","opts":["R","S","Q","P"],"ans":0,"exp":"Principle: a letter series behaves exactly like a number series once you convert each letter to its alphabet position — find the constant gap between positions, then step forward by that same gap. Step 1: convert each letter's alphabet position mentally and find the gap between consecutive letters — each moves forward by 2. Step 2: verify this holds for all consecutive pairs in H, J, L, N, P. Step 3: apply the same +2 shift to the last letter P: this gives R. Exam tip: the off-by-one distractors come from miscounting the shift by 1 in either direction — recount carefully rather than trusting a glance.","tags":["series"],"id":"R398"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"coding","diff":2,"q":"In a certain code, LAW is written as ODZ. How is EXAM written in that code?","opts":["GZCO","IBEQ","HADP","BUXJ"],"ans":2,"exp":"Principle: letter coding usually applies one consistent shift to every letter — find the rule from the known word pair, verify it holds across the whole word, then apply that exact same rule fresh to the new word. Step 1: compare LAW and ODZ letter by letter to find the shift — each letter moved by +3 positions. Step 2: verify on at least two letters (don't trust just one pair, since some ciphers change direction mid-word). Step 3: apply the confirmed +3 shift to every letter of EXAM: E→H X→A A→D M→P, giving HADP. Exam tip: negative shifts (moving backward) are easy to mistake for positive ones — double-check direction on the very first letter before committing to the whole word.","tags":["coding"],"id":"R399"},
{"sec":"A","topic":"operations","diff":2,"q":"If '+' means '×' and '−' means '÷', then 5 + 2 = ?","opts":["7","12","3","10"],"ans":3,"exp":"Principle: symbol-substitution questions swap the MEANING of operators, not the numbers — before calculating anything, rewrite the whole expression replacing every symbol with what it actually now stands for. Step 1: rewrite the expression using the REAL operation each symbol stands for — here '+' secretly means '×'. Step 2: the expression 5 + 2 becomes 5 × 2 once rewritten. Step 3: calculate: 5 × 2 = 10. The distractor 7 comes from forgetting to swap the symbol and adding normally instead. Exam tip: physically cross out each original symbol and write its replacement above it before doing any arithmetic — this stops your brain from defaulting to the familiar meaning.","tags":["operations"],"id":"R400"}
